::Q1::What is a streaming pipeline?
{ 
~A pipeline that cannot process incoming data
=A pipeline that processes data continuously as it is generated
~A pipeline that runs only once per year
~A pipeline that always waits for a complete daily batch
}

::Q2::Which use case is well suited to streaming? 
{
~A one-time historical data import
=Near-real-time fraud detection
~A daily business report that can wait for a scheduled batch
~A monthly archival process
}

::Q3::What should a pipeline do when a source row is missing a required field, according to the example implementation? 
{
~Delete the entire source file
~Automatically invent a value for the missing field
=Skip the row during the transformation step
~Stop all future pipeline runs permanently
}

::Q4::Why is scheduling important for many data pipelines? 
{
~It means a pipeline can run only once
=It allows pipelines to run automatically at the required frequency
~It prevents pipelines from processing data
~It removes the need for a destination
}

::Q5::How does a data pipeline relate to ETL and ELT? 
{
~It replaces extraction with manual reporting
~It means data no longer needs a destination
~It is unrelated to moving or transforming data
=It puts extraction, transformation, and loading concepts into an automated running process
}